---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';

/**
 * Written against what the code actually does, not from a template.
 *
 * Every row in the tables below corresponds to a real column in the server's
 * D1 schema or a real outbound call from its routes. If a table gains a column
 * that holds anything about a person, or a new third party starts receiving
 * data, this page is wrong until it is updated — that is the point of listing
 * the fields rather than saying "certain information".
 */
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="What UnityIDE collects, why, who it is shared with, and how to have it deleted."
  updated="2026-09-20"
>
  <p>
    UnityIDE is a desktop IDE for Unity developers, distributed from
    <a href="https://unityide.app">unityide.app</a>. This page explains exactly what
    the website and the desktop application collect, why, and who else sees it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The short version: we collect what is needed to run an account, bill a
    subscription, fix crashes, and answer your AI requests. We do not sell
    personal data.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>

  <h3>Account</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Data</th><th>Why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Email address</td>
          <td>Identifies the account, sends verification and password resets.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Password</td>
          <td>
            Stored only as a salted hash. We never store, log or transmit the
            password itself, and cannot recover it.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Plan</td>
          <td>Determines which features and usage limits apply.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    If you sign in with <strong>Google</strong> or <strong>GitHub</strong> instead,
    we receive your email address and basic profile information from that provider
    and create the same account record. We never receive your password for those
    services.
  </p>

  <h3>Payments</h3>
  <p>
    Subscriptions are processed by <strong>Dodo Payments</strong>, who act as the
    merchant of record. Card details are entered on their systems and are never
    sent to, seen by, or stored by us. We store only the subscription identifier,
    product, plan, status and renewal date needed to know what you are entitled to.
  </p>

  <h3>Desktop application</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Data</th><th>Why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Install identifier</td>
          <td>
            A random value generated on your machine on first run. It is not
            derived from your hardware, your account or any identifier assigned
            by your operating system, and it lets us count installs without
            counting the same machine twice.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Operating system, application version, release channel</td>
          <td>
            Tells us which builds are actually in use, so a platform is not
            broken for a release without anyone noticing.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Crash and error reports</td>
          <td>
            The error type and message, the stack trace, the application version,
            channel and operating system, a session identifier, and your user
            identifier when you are signed in. A stack trace can incidentally
            contain file paths from your project.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>AI features</h3>
  <p>
    This is the section worth reading closely, because it concerns your source code.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you use chat, inline completion or any other AI feature, your prompt is
    sent to a model provider together with the context needed to answer it. That
    context can include <strong>source files, file paths, symbol names, and
    information read from your open Unity Editor</strong> such as scene, prefab
    and asset structure. Requests are routed through Cloudflare AI Gateway to the
    model serving your plan; one model tier is served directly by Meta.
  </p>
  <p>
    We record the token counts and cost of each request in order to meter usage
    against your plan. If you do not want a project's code leaving your machine,
    do not use the AI features on that project.
  </p>

  <h3>Feedback</h3>
  <p>
    If you send feedback, we store your rating, message, chosen category and the
    email address you supply, so we can reply.
  </p>

  <h3>Website, advertising and security</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Ads and Google Tag Manager</strong> run on the public marketing
      and documentation pages to measure which ads lead to a download. They are
      never loaded on the sign-in, verification, password-reset, account or admin
      pages, because those URLs carry one-time tokens.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reddit</strong> advertising measurement works the same way. A click
      identifier from a Reddit ad is stored in a first-party cookie on this domain
      so that a later sign-up can be attributed to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare Turnstile</strong> protects the sign-in and sign-up forms
      from automated abuse.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>IP address and browser user agent</strong> are processed for security
      and abuse prevention, and are sent to Reddit in hashed or raw form as part of
      advertising conversion matching.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who else receives data</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Recipient</th><th>What they receive</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Cloudflare</td>
          <td>Hosting, database, file storage, AI gateway, bot protection. All data above passes through their infrastructure.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dodo Payments</td>
          <td>Your payment details and billing identity, as merchant of record.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Model providers</td>
          <td>AI prompts and the code context included with them.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Google</td>
          <td>Sign-in (if used), and advertising measurement events from the website.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>GitHub</td>
          <td>Sign-in, if used.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reddit</td>
          <td>Advertising conversion events, including a hashed email address where available.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>We do not sell personal data, and we do not share it for unrelated advertising.</p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    The website sets a session cookie so that you stay signed in, an advertising
    click-identifier cookie described above, and cookies set by Google's
    advertising tag. Blocking the advertising cookies does not affect your ability
    to use the site or the application.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Account and subscription records</strong> — for as long as the account exists.</li>
    <li><strong>Crash reports and install records</strong> — retained while they are useful for diagnosing a release, then deleted.</li>
    <li><strong>Advertising click identifiers</strong> — 30 days.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>AI prompts</strong> — not retained by us beyond what is needed to
      serve the request and record its token count. Model providers apply their
      own retention.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask us to show you the personal data we hold about you, correct it, or
    delete it along with your account. Write to
    <a href="mailto:support@unityide.app">support@unityide.app</a> from the address
    on the account and we will action it. Deleting your account removes the account
    record and unlinks it from crash reports; it does not retroactively withdraw
    data already processed by a payment provider, who have their own legal
    retention obligations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live you may have additional rights under local data
    protection law, including the right to object to processing and to complain to
    a supervisory authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    UnityIDE is a professional development tool and is not directed at children
    under 13. We do not knowingly collect their data.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes materially we will update the date at the top of this
    page. Continuing to use UnityIDE after a change means you accept the updated
    policy.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy, or a request about your data:
    <a href="mailto:support@unityide.app">support@unityide.app</a>. You can also use
    the <a href="/feedback">feedback form</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
